\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Why a positive Jacobian matters. A convex element has $\qopname \relax m{det}\bm {J}>0$ at every Gauss point; a re-entrant corner makes it non-positive and the stiffness wrong without an error message.\relax }}{11}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Four generated meshes: a hole off the centre of a rectangle with grading to a growth ratio (top left), a notch, a fillet, and a quarter plate with a hole. Produced by \texttt {scripts/ch02\_assets.py}.\relax }}{13}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Left: the largest ratio of adjacent element sizes of a 20-element power-law partition against the exponent. Right: the partition for a target growth ratio of 1.2, against a uniform one.\relax }}{13}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces A multi-block mesh: a quadrilateral and a triangle block over one shared node array. The interface nodes (red) belong to both blocks.\relax }}{14}{figure.caption.23}%
