\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.1}{\ignorespaces Idea of ECSW on a chain of $14$ elements (schematic; the weights are illustrative). Top: the full reduced force sums all elements with weight one. Bottom: a few elements with positive weights reproduce it; in the contact-aware variant the elements at potential contact are kept at weight one.\relax }}{125}{figure.caption.220}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.2}{\ignorespaces Reduction of a contact problem. The primal basis is a POD of displacement snapshots; the dual basis must be non-negative. The rank of the reduced constraint matrix is checked before the reduced LCP is solved; the dashed box shows the failure of a signed basis measured in \Cref {sec:hy-verif}.\relax }}{126}{figure.caption.222}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.3}{\ignorespaces The greedy loop of snapshot selection. The indicator needs only reduced solves, so scanning all candidates is cheap; the full-order model is called once per round.\relax }}{127}{figure.caption.224}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.4}{\ignorespaces Hybrid hyper-reduction on a reduced integration domain (schematic). Balance equations are written only at the dofs $P$ whose elements all lie in the RID; the contact forces on the RID are finite-element unknowns, so no dual basis is needed.\relax }}{127}{figure.caption.226}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.5}{\ignorespaces Linear and nonlinear zones. The linear interior is condensed onto the interface; the nonlinear problem is solved on the unknowns of $N$ (including $B$) and the interior is recovered afterwards.\relax }}{128}{figure.caption.228}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.6}{\ignorespaces Left: centre displacement for an obstacle outside the training range, full order and reduced with the NMF dual basis. Middle: trajectory error against $p=p_\lambda $. Right: smallest lifted multiplier for the NMF and SVD dual bases (held-out outside obstacle).\relax }}{130}{figure.caption.231}%
